WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250 align:start position:0%
 
who's our home affairs correspondent  mark white has been on a boat in the  English Channel today and witnessed  already seven different incidents of  migrants attempting to get into the UK  this comes as the government has  promised to get tough on migrant  crossings as hundreds of people have  been intercepted during the coronavirus  lockdown mark white joins me now live  from Dover mark thank you for being with  us tell us about what you've seen  well Adam we saw some significant  rescues underway in the English Channel  overnight and into this morning  it is meteor problem of course according  to the Home Secretary pretty Patel who  is diverting extra resources to this  side of the channel and to her  colleagues in France with a view to  trying to intercept these boats before  they can even leave the French course  well what we saw heading into the night  at 2:00 a.m. this morning into the  pitch-black into the middle of the  channel was the first of a number of  rescues that happened actually just as  dawn broke over the busy shipping lanes  out into the channel we saw this migrant  boat 13 men and women on board that  vessel and it was actually being  shadowed by a French patrol board now it  had been shadowed pretty much from the  moment it left the beach in France that  vessel was with it for some two hours as  it made slow progress towards the  English side of the English Channel that  is of course the goal of these migrants  to come across here to claim asylum when  they get to the English side once  they're taken to Dover we as I say saw  the French boat out there then the  border force vessel as well this is a  flavour of what we witnessed we can see  the French patrol boat Aramis circling  the area while the border force vessel  speed well is now down in front of the  migrants port they just got them to stop  their engines they're about to try  to get the migrants from that ball  transferred onto their vessel now there  is some concern that French authorities  may not be overly enthusiastic in their  efforts to try to intercept these  vessels on the French side of the  English Channel and are perhaps more  than happy for them to hand into British  waters before border force here in the  UK intercept them this is what we heard  from Matt Coker who is the skipper of  the boat we were on to be honest that's  not the first time I've seen that as  quite regular they don't seem so  interested in stopping the vessels as  they do so they scored them across yeah  and but it intercepted that vessel in  French waters it did yeah just a few  miles from the shore so I mean it was a  few hours passed before they reached the  actual border in the middle of the  channel  well the border for special speed well  intercepted those migrants and fairly  quickly was able to take them on board  they were clearly at that point willing  to shut down their engine according to  sources we've spoken to the issue really  is that migrants just are not compliant  with the French because the goal is to  reach the English side of the channel  and to be taken to Dover so once the  border force are on scene they will then  switch the engine off and they're  willing to board the board these border  force vessels now that particular border  force vessels Speedwell was heading back  into Dover when it got too cold to  another rescue it headed off to pick up  another six migrants and then entered  Dover Harbour with 19 migrants on board  at the same time as that was happening  the Dover lifeboat was out picking up  two separate groups of migrants another  border force vessel the cutter searcher  and another vessel the border force boat  hunter were also out picking migrants up  in separate incidents  sixty-five we managed to count them may  well have been more in the time that we  have been back on shore again but  another significant day and really a  repetition of what happened yesterday  with some 64 migrants who've crossed the  channel as I say the Home Secretary Adam  says that they are investing more money  it's a top priority for them they say  the French are on board that the French  are as committed to trying to stop them  getting off the beaches in the first  place but it's clear when they're in the  water the migrants clearly don't want to  stop they want to continue until they  get in to the English side of the  channel and then are intercepted by the  UK border force now what is the legal  position on this because Nigel Farage  has also been out reporting or alleging  that the French are in fact assisting  people crossing the channel into the UK  what do you understand the international  law the French should be doing about  these boats well if they are in French  territorial waters then the legal  position is quite clear Adam and that is  the French of a duty to try to intercept  pick up these migrants and return them  to France  home of a spokesman said that they have  done that the French authorities have  been doing that and in the past week or  two you have returned 155 migrants back  to France but the issue they are coming  up against is that when you have  migrants not willing to comply going a  different course that the French patrol  boats are trying to make them go and not  switching off their engines the French  then have no option but to continue  shadowing because they have a duty in  terms of a safety to life duty that they  have as well so they stay within easy  reach of the vessel should it run into  trouble but if it's not going to switch  off its engine they're not going to run  a small rubber dinghy like that  effectively just waiting for it to cross  into this side of the channel and for  border forces to come and intercept it

